<h2>Why Hiring the Right Contractor Matters More Than You Think</h2><p>A fresh coat of paint or a well-done repair can add real value to your home. A bad contractor can do the opposite — leaving you with shoddy work, surprise costs, or worse, a legal headache. In Western Massachusetts, where harsh winters and humid summers put homes through their paces, the stakes are especially high. Before you sign anything, make sure you are not making one of these common mistakes.</p><h2>Mistake 1: Skipping License and Insurance Verification</h2><figure class="blog-figure"><img src="/images/stock/web/blog-6-mistakes-to-avoid-when-hiring-a-home-services-contractor-in-massachusetts-2.jpg" alt="Home maintenance work underway on a New England property" width="1100" height="619" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></figure><p>Massachusetts requires most home improvement contractors to register with the Office of Consumer Affairs and Business Regulation. Hiring someone who is not registered puts you at risk if something goes wrong. Beyond registration, always confirm the contractor carries general liability insurance and workers' compensation coverage. If a worker is injured on your property and the contractor has no coverage, you could be held responsible. Ask for certificates of insurance and verify them directly with the insurer — do not just take a contractor's word for it.</p><h2>Mistake 2: Choosing Based on Price Alone</h2><p>Getting multiple quotes is smart. Automatically choosing the lowest one is not. An unusually low bid often signals that a contractor plans to cut corners on materials, skip prep work, or add charges later through change orders. When comparing quotes, look at what is actually included — surface preparation, number of coats, material quality, cleanup, and warranty terms. A slightly higher price from a thorough, reputable contractor almost always saves money in the long run.</p><h3>Watch Out for Vague Estimates</h3><p>A written estimate should spell out every line item. If a quote just says 'paint two rooms' without specifying brand, sheen, prep steps, or number of coats, push back. Vague estimates leave room for dispute once work begins.</p><div class="blog-cta-inline"><p>Would you rather we handled it? Estimates are always free.</p><a class="btn btn--gold" href="/contact.html#estimate-form">Get a Free Estimate</a></div><h2>Mistake 3: Not Checking References and Reviews</h2><p>Word of mouth still matters, especially in tightly knit communities like Westfield, Southampton, and Agawam. Ask every contractor for at least three references from jobs completed in the past year, and actually call them. Ask whether the work was finished on time, whether the final cost matched the estimate, and whether they would hire that contractor again. Online reviews on Google and the Better Business Bureau can fill in the gaps, but direct references give you the clearest picture.</p><h2>Mistake 4: Ignoring Lead Paint Rules on Older Homes</h2><p>Massachusetts has a large stock of pre-1978 housing, particularly in cities like Springfield, Holyoke, and West Springfield. If your home was built before 1978, any contractor disturbing painted surfaces must follow federal lead-safe work practices. For homes built before 1978, <a href="https://www.epa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">the EPA</a> sets the rules for disturbing lead paint, including requirements for certified renovators and specific containment procedures. Hiring a contractor who ignores these rules does not just put your family at risk — it can also expose you to legal liability as the property owner.</p><h3>Ask About Certification</h3><p>Before any scraping, sanding, or demolition begins on an older home, confirm that the contractor holds EPA Renovation, Repair, and Painting (RRP) certification. This is not optional — it is the law.</p><h2>Mistake 5: Signing a Weak or Incomplete Contract</h2><p>A handshake deal is not enough, no matter how trustworthy someone seems. Every home services job should be backed by a written contract that includes the full scope of work, materials to be used, a payment schedule, a project timeline, and what happens if something goes wrong. Massachusetts law actually requires written contracts for home improvement jobs over $1,000. Read the contract carefully before signing. If anything is unclear, ask for clarification in writing. Never pay the full amount upfront — a standard structure is a deposit to start, progress payments tied to milestones, and a final payment only after you are satisfied with the completed work. <a href="/services.html">the services we offer</a> for more guidance on what a complete project agreement should cover.</p><h2>Mistake 6: Underestimating Seasonal Timing</h2><p>New England's climate directly affects the quality and longevity of many home services, particularly exterior painting and any work involving caulk, adhesives, or sealants. Most exterior coatings need temperatures consistently above 50 degrees Fahrenheit to cure properly. Scheduling exterior work in late fall or early spring in Western Massachusetts can result in adhesion failures, peeling, and premature wear. Plan ahead. Book exterior projects for late spring through early fall, and confirm with your contractor that they will monitor conditions and reschedule if a cold snap or heavy rain is forecast. <a href="https://www.nahb.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">the National Association of Home Builders</a> notes that proper application conditions are a key factor in whether exterior finishes meet their rated lifespan.</p><section class="blog-faq"><h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>Do Massachusetts contractors need to be licensed?</h3><p>Massachusetts requires home improvement contractors to register with the state's Office of Consumer Affairs and Business Regulation, but 'licensed' and 'registered' mean different things. Always verify your contractor's registration status before any work begins. Specialty trades like electricians and plumbers carry separate licensing requirements.</p><h3>How much should I pay upfront to a contractor?</h3><p>A reasonable deposit is typically 10 to 30 percent of the total project cost. Never pay the full amount before work is complete, and tie progress payments to specific milestones spelled out in your contract. Contractors who demand full payment upfront are a red flag.</p><h3>What should I do if a contractor damages my property?</h3><p>Document the damage immediately with photos and notify the contractor in writing. If they carry proper liability insurance, you can file a claim through their insurer. If the issue is not resolved, Massachusetts homeowners can file a complaint with the Office of Consumer Affairs and Business Regulation.</p><h3>Is it safe to hire a contractor found through social media or flyers?</h3><p>It can be, but take the same precautions you would with any contractor — verify registration, check insurance, ask for references, and insist on a written contract. Being found on social media does not indicate legitimacy one way or the other.</p><h3>How do I know if my home has lead paint?</h3><p>Homes built before 1978 may contain lead-based paint. A certified lead inspector or risk assessor can test your home before any renovation work begins. This step is especially important in older neighborhoods throughout Holyoke, Springfield, and West Springfield.</p></section>
